[38;5;252m[1m## The bug I had to reproduce, not fix[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10msrc/spectral_contrast.c[0m includes only [38;5;10m<math.h>[0m — never [38;5;10mmatch.h[0m. So its [38;5;10mfloat_t[0m is C99's [38;5;10mfloat_t[0m from [38;5;10m<math.h>[0m, which on x86-64 glibc ([38;5;10m__FLT_EVAL_METHOD__ == 0[0m) is [1m[38;5;10mfloat[0m[22m, not the [38;5;10mtypedef double float_t[0m from [38;5;10mmatch.h[0m. I confirmed this in the compiled object: [38;5;10mspectral_contrast[0m uses [38;5;10mmovss[0m/[38;5;10mmulss[0m with a 4-byte stride, while [38;5;10mmatch[0m uses [38;5;10mmovsd[0m with an 8-byte stride.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
So [38;5;10mmatch[0m builds [38;5;10mdouble[0m scratch buffers and hands them to a function that reads them as [38;5;10mfloat[0m — reinterpreting the low half of each buffer and writing [38;5;10mf32[0m results back over those bytes. The Rust does exactly the same reinterpretation. The practical effect is that [38;5;10mspectral_contrast[0m returns near-zero garbage for real spectra, which the linked-binary test shows: [38;5;10mmatch(thr=0.25)[0m returns 0 even for two nearly identical spectra.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m## The one real translation hazard[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The first version compiled and passed most inputs but diverged on NaN. [38;5;10mADDSD dst, src[0m and [38;5;10mMULSS dst, src[0m return the [1mdestination[22m operand's payload when both are NaN, and since [38;5;10mfadd[0m/[38;5;10mfmul[0m are commutative, the compiler picks which operand becomes the destination. GCC keeps the accumulator in the destination register ([38;5;10maddsd 0x8(%rdx),%xmm0[0m); LLVM picks the freshly loaded value instead. Result: C propagated the [3mfirst[23m NaN in an array, Rust the [3mlast[23m. I added [38;5;10msrc/fp.rs[0m, which pins the operand roles by emulating the SSE rule explicitly. Non-commutative ops ([38;5;10msubsd[0m, [38;5;10mdivsd[0m, [38;5;10msqrtsd[0m) needed no helper — their destination is fixed by source order.[0m[0m
